    "result_text": "A claim now belongs to the period that contains its due-by date instead of its date. All 4 acceptance tests pass with `bin/e2e`.\n\nNo change to the stored data was needed: each claim's due-by is already saved when the claim is created. What changed is everything that worked out a claim's period from its date.\n\n**Server**\n- **New helper:** `claim/DueBy.java` holds the due-by rule: the date plus the person's own terms, otherwise their grade's terms, otherwise the sheet's. Adding a claim, splitting one and the closed-period checks on new claims all use it. It had to be its own class because OfficeFloor won't start if a step class has more than one public method.\n- **Closed periods:** opening, settling, voiding, editing, re-dating, bulk open and bulk settle are now refused when the claim's due-by falls in a closed period. For a claim about to be added, the check uses the date plus the terms it would get. This covers the claim form, paste and quick-add. For a quick-add with a name new to the sheet, it uses grade A terms, since that's the grade the new person gets.\n- **Numbering and period totals:** per-period numbers and the totals fixed when a period is closed or soft-closed now go by due-by.\n- **Late entry:** a claim is flagged as a late entry when its due-by is in a soft-closed period. Split-off claims now get this flag too; before, they never did. The test expects it.\n\n**Front end**\n- The period shown on a claim, and the per-person and per-period totals, now use due-by (`periods.ts`, `claims.slot.tsx`).\n\n**Behaviour changes to know about**\n- **Re-dating a claim keeps its number.** Due-by doesn't change after creation, so a re-date can't move a claim to another period. It used to take a new period number.\n- **Editing and re-dating check only due-by.** They no longer look at the new date, for the same reason.\n\nThe test only re-dates a claim while numbering by period is off, so the first change isn't covered by it.",
